---
/**
 * CanMayMatrix — CAN / MAY × WITHIN-WORLD / CROSSING. The page's principal synthesis (brief §3).
 *
 * WHAT IS CLAIMED, EXACTLY. Neither axis is presented as newly discovered, and no historical
 * priority is claimed. The safe and true claim, stated on the page: datumwise elevates within-world
 * versus crossing into an ORGANIZING AXIS for analytical governance, and crosses it explicitly with
 * capability versus legitimacy. That is a claim about organization, not about invention.
 *
 * NOT FOUR PRODUCT BOXES. The matrix is an explanatory instrument. Its cells are questions, not
 * features, and the figure exists to make one consequence unavoidable: capability does not
 * establish legitimacy, and that survives on both sides of a boundary.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS NOT A <table>. A 2×2 of full sentences cannot hold two readable columns at 375px, so
 * it has to linearize — and `display: block` on table parts strips the very row/column header
 * association that made the table worth using. So the axis coordinate of every cell is REAL DOM
 * TEXT in the cell's own heading ("Within-world · Capability — can"), present and correct at every
 * width, with no dependency on CSS or JavaScript. The rails around the grid are the desktop
 * READING of that structure and are aria-hidden, because they would otherwise repeat the headings.
 *
 * DOM order is the accessible reading order the brief requires:
 *   axis definitions → within-world can → within-world may → crossing can → crossing may.
 */
const CELLS = [
  {
    id: 'within-can',
    place: 'Within-world',
    question: 'Capability — can',
    text: 'What can be computed or derived inside the jurisdiction?',
  },
  {
    id: 'within-may',
    place: 'Within-world',
    question: 'Legitimacy — may',
    text: "What is lawful under that world's own law?",
  },
  {
    id: 'crossing-can',
    place: 'Crossing',
    question: 'Capability — can',
    text: 'What can be transported, invoked, or executed across the boundary?',
  },
  {
    id: 'crossing-may',
    place: 'Crossing',
    question: 'Legitimacy — may',
    text: 'What is licensed to pass, with the required warrant and authority?',
  },
];
---

<section id="can-may" class="matrix-section ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="matrix-h">
  <div class="ds-measure-wide">
    <h2 id="matrix-h" class="ds-label">Two axes: capability and legitimacy, interior and crossing</h2>

    <p class="ds-proposition">Capability answers <em>can</em>. Governance must still answer <em>may</em>.</p>

    <div class="axes">
      <div class="axis">
        <h3 class="axis-h">Axis 1 &mdash; source of law</h3>
        <p class="axis-pair"><span>within-world</span> <span class="vs">vs</span> <span>crossing</span></p>
        <p class="axis-def ds-body">
          A world's <strong>interior</strong> is where its own law has jurisdiction. A
          <strong>crossing</strong> is an operation that leaves one jurisdiction for another, and the
          <strong>boundary</strong> is where that happens. The two are governed differently because
          different law applies.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="axis">
        <h3 class="axis-h">Axis 2 &mdash; governance question</h3>
        <p class="axis-pair"><span>capability &mdash; can</span> <span class="vs">vs</span> <span>legitimacy &mdash; may</span></p>
        <p class="axis-def ds-body">
          <em>Can</em> is what the machinery is able to do. <em>May</em> is not merely runtime
          permission: it includes analytical law, warrant, and authority. A system that has collapsed
          the second into the first has not simplified governance; it has removed it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="matrix">
      <div class="rail rail-col rail-can" aria-hidden="true"><span>Capability &mdash; can</span></div>
      <div class="rail rail-col rail-may" aria-hidden="true"><span>Legitimacy &mdash; may</span></div>
      <div class="rail rail-row rail-within" aria-hidden="true"><span>Within-world</span></div>
      <div class="rail rail-row rail-crossing" aria-hidden="true"><span>Crossing</span></div>

      {CELLS.map((c) => (
        <article class={`cell cell-${c.id}`} id={`cell-${c.id}`}>
          <h3 class="coord">
            <span class="coord-place">{c.place}</span>
            <span class="coord-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
            <span class="coord-question">{c.question}</span>
          </h3>
          <p class="cell-text">{c.text}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="after">
      <div class="consequence">
        <p class="cons-label">The consequence</p>
        <p class="cons-line">Lawfulness first, execution second.</p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          The matrix is an explanatory instrument, not four independent product boxes. Read across it
          and the same fact appears twice: being able to compute a quantity inside a world does not
          make it lawful there, and being able to transport a result across a boundary does not
          license its passage.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body claim-scope">
          The claim here is about organization, not discovery. datumwise elevates within-world versus
          crossing into an organizing axis for analytical governance and crosses it explicitly with
          capability versus legitimacy. Neither distinction is claimed as new, and no historical
          priority is claimed for the pairing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="sovereignty" aria-labelledby="sov-h">
        <h3 id="sov-h" class="sov-h">The sovereignty guard</h3>
        <blockquote class="sov-quote">
          <p>It supplies the interior law of no world it composes. It has wider scope, not higher sovereignty.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="ds-body">
          The figure must not be read as Analytical Governance supplying every world's law. It tracks
          which law has jurisdiction, whether an operation is interior or a crossing, and what warrant
          and authority survive each passage. It does not legislate inside the worlds it coordinates.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          A world's interior is where its own law has jurisdiction &mdash; and the lawful operation
          space inside that jurisdiction may still be <em>partial</em>. That matters more than it
          looks: <strong>a totalized interior cannot refuse.</strong> A world in which every
          expressible operation is automatically lawful has no way to withhold anything, and by the
          category's own membership test it is not governed.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          Nor is <em>world</em> a decorative name for every stage in a pipeline. In particular,
          <strong>evidence is not a sovereign world</strong>: it is a standing acquired through a
          governed crossing. Constituting an &ldquo;evidence world&rdquo; to make a diagram
          symmetrical would invent a jurisdiction to fill a quadrant.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .ds-proposition { max-width: 20ch; margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-4); }
  .ds-proposition em { font-style: italic; }

  .axes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 3vw, 4rem);
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-5);
    padding-bottom: var(--ds-space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
  }
  .axis-h {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .axis-pair {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--ds-mono);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .axis-pair .vs { color: var(--ink-faint); padding: 0 0.35rem; }
  .axis-def { margin: 0; max-width: 46ch; }
  .axis-def strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .axis-def em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }

  /* ── THE MATRIX ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Rails in the first row/column, four cells in the body. The rails are decoration for the
     desktop reading; every cell states its own coordinate in the DOM. */
  .matrix {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(6rem, 0.42fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--ds-rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--ds-rule);
  }
  .rail, .cell { background: var(--paper); }

  .rail-col { grid-row: 1; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; }
  .rail-can { grid-column: 2; }
  .rail-may { grid-column: 3; }
  .rail-row { grid-column: 1; padding: 1.3rem 1.1rem; }
  .rail-within { grid-row: 2; }
  .rail-crossing { grid-row: 3; }
  .rail span {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* the empty corner */
  .matrix::before { content: ''; grid-area: 1 / 1; background: var(--paper); }

  .cell { padding: 1.3rem 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem; align-content: start; }
  .cell-within-can { grid-area: 2 / 2; }
  .cell-within-may { grid-area: 2 / 3; }
  .cell-crossing-can { grid-area: 3 / 2; }
  .cell-crossing-may { grid-area: 3 / 3; }

  /* The coordinate heading is the cell's real axis identity, and it is ALWAYS in the DOM.
     On wide screens the rails already state both axes, so repeating them in every cell is visual
     noise — the heading is therefore visually hidden here, not deleted. It stays available to
     assistive technology, to a reader with CSS off, and to any machine reading the page, which is
     the whole reason it exists. Below the linearization breakpoint it becomes the primary label. */
  .coord {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }
  .cell-text {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--ds-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .after {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5rem);
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-5);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .cons-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .cons-line {
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--ds-pillar);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .consequence .ds-body { margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2); max-width: 50ch; }
  .claim-scope { color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: 0.97rem; }

  .sovereignty {
    padding-left: clamp(1rem, 0.5rem + 1.5vw, 2rem);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--ds-rule-strong);
  }
  .sov-h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--ds-meta);
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .sov-quote { margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2); }
  .sov-quote p {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--ds-lead);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .sovereignty .ds-body { margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-2); max-width: 46ch; }
  .sovereignty .ds-body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .sovereignty strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .sovereignty em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }

  /* ── LINEARIZED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The two axes must remain understandable. The rails become full-width band headings that keep
     the row axis visible, and every cell's own coordinate heading is promoted to primary — so a
     narrow reader still sees "Within-world · Capability — can", never four loose cards. */
  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .matrix { grid-template-columns: 1fr; grid-template-rows: none; }
    .matrix::before { display: none; }
    .rail-col { display: none; }
    .rail-row, .cell { grid-area: auto !important; grid-column: 1 !important; }
    .rail-row { padding: 0.85rem 1.1rem; background: var(--paper-raised); }
    /* linearized: the cell's own coordinate becomes its visible label, so the two axes survive
       the loss of the grid */
    .coord {
      position: static;
      width: auto;
      height: auto;
      margin: 0;
      overflow: visible;
      clip-path: none;
      white-space: normal;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--ds-mono-sm);
      font-weight: 400;
      letter-spacing: 0.02em;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0 0.4rem;
    }
    /* source order already places both within-world cells before both crossing cells, so the
       bands land in the right places without reordering the DOM */
    .rail-within { order: 1; } .cell-within-can { order: 2; } .cell-within-may { order: 3; }
    .rail-crossing { order: 4; } .cell-crossing-can { order: 5; } .cell-crossing-may { order: 6; }
    .matrix { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .axes { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--ds-space-3); }
    .after { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--ds-space-4); }
    .sovereignty { padding-left: 0; border-left: 0; padding-top: var(--ds-space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-rule); }
    .ds-proposition { max-width: none; }
  }
</style>
